### What is gRPC?

gRPC is a high-performance, contract-first Remote Procedure Call framework that runs over HTTP/2 and serializes messages with Protocol Buffers. You define services and messages once in a `.proto` file, and a code generator produces client stubs and server base classes.

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";
package com.bank.grpc;

option java_package = "com.bank.grpc";

service AccountService {
  rpc Transfer (TransferRequest) returns (TransferResponse);
  rpc GetStatement (StatementRequest) returns (stream Transaction);
}

message TransferRequest {
  string from_account = 1;
  string to_account = 2;
  string amount = 3;  // String for BigDecimal safety
}

message TransferResponse {
  bool success = 1;
  string new_balance = 2;
  string message = 3;
}

message StatementRequest {
  string account_id = 1;
  int32 month = 2;
}

message Transaction {
  string id = 1;
  string amount = 2;
  string description = 3;
  int64 timestamp = 4;
}
```

### How do you use gRPC in Java?

The Protocol Buffers compiler generates client stubs and server base classes. Calling a remote method feels like calling a local one.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import io.grpc.stub.StreamObserver;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountGrpcService
        extends AccountServiceGrpc.AccountServiceImplBase {

    @Override
    public void transfer(
            TransferRequest request,
            StreamObserver<TransferResponse> observer) {

        BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal(request.getAmount());
        String from = request.getFromAccount();
        String to = request.getToAccount();

        // Rajesh transfers 50000 to Priya
        boolean success = processTransfer(from, to, amount);

        observer.onNext(TransferResponse.newBuilder()
                .setSuccess(success)
                .setNewBalance("200000.00")
                .setMessage("Transfer complete")
                .build());
        observer.onCompleted();
    }

    private boolean processTransfer(
            String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Transfer " + amount
                + " from " + from + " to " + to);
        return true;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.client;

import com.bank.grpc.AccountServiceGrpc;
import com.bank.grpc.TransferRequest;
import com.bank.grpc.TransferResponse;
import io.grpc.ManagedChannel;
import io.grpc.ManagedChannelBuilder;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferClient {

    public void executeTransfer() {
        ManagedChannel channel = ManagedChannelBuilder
                .forAddress("localhost", 9090)
                .usePlaintext()
                .build();

        AccountServiceGrpc.AccountServiceBlockingStub stub =
                AccountServiceGrpc.newBlockingStub(channel);

        TransferResponse response = stub.transfer(
                TransferRequest.newBuilder()
                        .setFromAccount("SAV2001")
                        .setToAccount("SAV3001")
                        .setAmount("50000.00")
                        .build());

        System.out.println("Success: " + response.getSuccess());
        System.out.println("Balance: " + response.getNewBalance());
        channel.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### What are the four call types?

gRPC defines four method kinds that cover different communication patterns.

| Call Type | Request | Response | Use Case |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unary | One | One | Transfer, balance lookup |
| Server streaming | One | Stream | Statement feed, price feed |
| Client streaming | Stream | One | Bulk upload, aggregation |
| Bidirectional | Stream | Stream | Real-time chat, live trading |

```java
// Server streaming: statement feed
@Override
public void getStatement(
        StatementRequest request,
        StreamObserver<Transaction> observer) {

    String accountId = request.getAccountId();
    // Stream transaction entries one by one
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
        observer.onNext(Transaction.newBuilder()
                .setId("TXN-" + i)
                .setAmount("1000.00")
                .setDescription("Monthly EMI payment")
                .build());
    }
    observer.onCompleted();
}
```

### What are the advantages of gRPC?

High performance and low bandwidth from binary Protocol Buffers. Strong typing and a single source-of-truth contract that generates clients in many languages. Built-in streaming. First-class deadlines, cancellation, and metadata. Excellent fit for polyglot microservices.

### What are the disadvantages of gRPC?

Not human-readable like JSON, so debugging needs tooling (grpcurl, reflection). Limited native browser support, requiring gRPC-Web and a proxy. Requires HTTP/2 end to end, which some older proxies handle poorly. Steeper learning curve and build-time code generation.

### How is gRPC different from REST? (Comparison)

| Feature | gRPC | REST |
|---|---|---|
| Protocol | HTTP/2 | HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 |
| Serialization | Protocol Buffers (binary) | JSON (text) |
| Contract | .proto file (enforced) | OpenAPI/Swagger (optional) |
| Streaming | Native (4 types) | Limited (SSE, WebSocket) |
| Browser support | gRPC-Web + proxy | Native |
| Human-readable | No | Yes |
| Code generation | Required | Optional |
| Style | Method/action-oriented RPC | Resource-oriented |
| Schema evolution | Field numbers | Version headers |

### Why use Protocol Buffers instead of JSON?

Protocol Buffers serialize to a compact binary form using field numbers, which is far smaller and faster to parse than JSON. The numbered fields enable safe schema evolution: adding a new field does not break old clients.

```java
// JSON: ~120 bytes, text parsing
// { "from_account": "SAV2001", "to_account": "SAV3001",
//   "amount": "50000.00" }

// Protocol Buffers: ~35 bytes, binary parsing
// Field 1: SAV2001, Field 2: SAV3001, Field 3: 50000.00
// Numbered tags = compact, evolutionable
```

### When should you use gRPC vs REST?

Use gRPC for internal east-west microservice communication where performance and a shared contract matter. Use REST for public, browser-facing, or partner APIs where ubiquity and readability win. Put a REST or gRPC-Web gateway in front of internal gRPC services when the edge needs it.

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│            Architecture Rule              │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                          │
│  Browser/Partner ──REST──→ API Gateway   │
│                              │            │
│                         gRPC-Web proxy    │
│                              │            │
│  Internal: Service ──gRPC──→ Service     │
│            (fast, typed, streaming)       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Money should be carried as a string in the proto and parsed into BigDecimal, never using floating point. Use deadlines on every call to prevent unbounded waits. The proto file is the single shared contract, so keep it versioned and use field number discipline for evolution. For debugging, use grpcurl or enable reflection.
